-> command_failed` branch, so the subclass check is not shadowed.","cohort":["T8"],"criteria":["T8"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.365,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'GateCongestedError is defined … as a direct subclass of LLMError (not of LLMRunnerError)'","Plan: '_errors._llm_error_code gets one isinstance(exc, GateCongestedError) branch returning a new \"gate_congested\" code'","_errors.py _llm_error_code ends with `if isinstance(exc, LLMError): return \"command_failed\"` (line 129) — a GateCongestedError is an LLMError, so a branch added AFTER this line never fires","AC3b requires error == 'gate_congested'; shadowing would emit 'command_failed' instead"],"finding":"The plan adds a gate_congested branch to _llm_error_code but does not specify it must precede the existing bare `isinstance(exc, LLMError) -> command_failed` branch, so the new decision-rule arm's placement is ambiguous.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f9345ace4f728fe91","impact":0.536,"is_primary":null,"location":"Design → 'Refusal — the exact hierarchy' / 'Vocabulary' bullet; src/rebar/_errors.py:109-131","norm_id":"nd369045046b5f0a1","priority":0.335,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A congestion refusal reaches the MCP body; error_code_for/_llm_error_code classifies the GateCongestedError","AC3b assertion that the MCP body returns error == 'gate_congested'"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"State in the Vocabulary bullet that the gate_congested branch is inserted ahead of the terminal `isinstance(exc, LLMError)` arm in `_llm_error_code` (e.g. immediately after the LLMUnavailableError check), and note that the taxonomy contract's by-type test (error_code_for on a bare instance) covers this ordering.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.625,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"medium"}}},{"block_threshold":0.9,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State the intended behavior of the concurrency cap on platforms without fcntl (Windows): confirm fail-open is deliberate for that shape and note the cap is a no-op there, or bound scope to POSIX hosts explicitly.","cohort":["E3","E5","G5","T5b","T6","T9","necessity","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.portability"],"criteria":["project.portability"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.6937,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'each held for the run under a non-blocking fcntl.flock(LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB)'","'Every plumbing failure (no fcntl, unwritable store) fails OPEN.'","fcntl is a POSIX-only Python module absent on Windows; the CLI is a supported shape on Windows workstations per the matrix"],"finding":"The concurrency cap relies on fcntl.flock, which is unavailable on Windows; the plan's 'no fcntl fails OPEN' rule means the bound silently becomes a no-op there, so the disk-fill protection the ticket exists to provide does not apply on that supported platform.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"ff882f7844bd0dfc3","impact":0.33,"is_primary":null,"location":"Design — Counter / 'Every plumbing failure (no fcntl, unwritable store) fails OPEN.'","norm_id":"n78142b7ddc118827","priority":0.2063,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Windows workstation running rebar as CLI: fcntl import fails, admission fails open, gates run unbounded — the ~10-parallel disk-fill shape S6 targets recurs with no cap and no error, a silent no-op the operator cannot observe as a cap-disabled state"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Add a sentence to the Counter/portability note stating the cap is enforced only on POSIX hosts (the t4g.large gate host) and is a deliberate no-op on Windows CLI, so the fail-open is not mistaken for an enforced bound.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.625,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"low","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] In Design → Counter, add one sentence stating why a named POSIX semaphore (posix_ipc, a new dependency) or multiprocessing.Semaphore (not crash-safe across independently-launched processes — no auto-release on SIGKILL, contradicting AC5) is rejected in favour of reusing the repo's existing fcntl.flock primitive on N slot files.","cohort":["T1"],"criteria":["T1"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.6057,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan text: 'N slot files in the snapshot store's existing locks/ directory, each held ... under a non-blocking fcntl.flock(LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB)'","multiprocessing.Semaphore EXISTS in the environment but is not crash-safe across independently-launched processes (does not auto-release on holder SIGKILL) — which is precisely AC4/AC5's requirement; posix_ipc is not installed (would be a new dependency)","The plan grounds crash-safety on the kernel dropping a flock on holder death (AC5), corroborated by retrieved man7 flock(2) and other sources — the exact property the stdlib alternatives lack","This is anti-repackaging in substance (it reuses ADR 0005's locks/ dir and the repo's fcntl.flock primitive), so the gap is a one-line rationale omission, not a novel-architecture defect"],"finding":"The plan justifies its flock-slot-file semaphore against the rejected heartbeat-lease and the deferred env-var mechanism, but does not explicitly contrast it with the more obvious stdlib/library concurrency primitives (multiprocessing.Semaphore, a named POSIX semaphore via posix_ipc) that a reader would reach for first.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fea1227af54e82bc3","impact":0.33,"is_primary":null,"location":"Design → Counter","norm_id":"n1e83153eec56636b","priority":0.0943,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A reviewer or future maintainer asks why a bespoke slot-file counter was built instead of a named POSIX semaphore or multiprocessing.Semaphore"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Add: 'A named POSIX semaphore (posix_ipc) is a new dependency and multiprocessing.Semaphore does not auto-release on a killed holder (AC5), so neither meets the cross-process + crash-safe bar; the flock-slot-file counter reuses the store's existing, tested primitive instead.'","tier":"LLM","validity":0.2857,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","comm